<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Montreal</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Montreal is the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, on an island in the St Lawrence River. Founded as Ville-Marie in 1642 by French Catholic settlers, it became the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a> and surrendered to the British in 1760. Briefly capital of the Province of Canada in the 1840s, it was the economic capital of Canada for much of its history and remains a major French-speaking city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/montreal.jpg" alt="Montreal" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Quintin Soloviev · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Montreal,_Quebec_skyline.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Montreal lies in the southwest of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>. The city covers most of the Island of Montreal, at the confluence of the Saint Lawrence and Ottawa Rivers, between the Saint Lawrence to the south and the Rivière des Prairies to the north. It is 196 km east of Ottawa and 258 km southwest of Quebec City. Its port lies at one end of the Saint Lawrence Seaway, the river gateway that stretches from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> to the Atlantic. The most prominent feature of the island is Mount Royal, a mountain with three heads that rises 232 metres above sea level.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeological evidence shows that First Nations peoples occupied the island as early as 4,000 years ago. By the year 1000 they had begun to cultivate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, and within a few hundred years they had built fortified villages. The Saint Lawrence Iroquoians, a group distinct from the Haudenosaunee of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, established the village of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hochelaga">Hochelaga</a> at the foot of Mount Royal two centuries before the French arrived. The French explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-cartier">Jacques Cartier</a> visited Hochelaga on 2 October 1535 and estimated its people at over a thousand. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> language the land is called Tiohtià:ke, loosely where the group divided; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ojibwe">Ojibwe</a> it is Mooniyaang, the first stopping place in the Ojibwe migration story.</p><p>Settlers from La Flèche in the Loire Valley founded the town in 1642 as Ville Marie, named for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a>. The present name is generally thought to derive from Mount Royal, since réal and royal were used interchangeably in 16th-century French. Another explanation points to the Venetian geographer Giovanni Battista Ramusio, who wrote Monte Real on his 1556 map, but the Commission de toponymie du Québec disputes it. For over a century and a half Montreal was the industrial and financial centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a> surpassed it in population and economic strength in the 1970s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>French is the official language of Montreal, which is the largest primarily French speaking city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and one of the most bilingual in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. The city is divided into 19 boroughs, many of which were independent cities until a municipal reorganisation in January 2002. It holds 50 National Historic Sites of Canada, more than any other city, many of them factories, warehouses and mills from its industrial past. Montreal hosted the 1967 International and Universal Exposition and the 1976 Summer Olympics, and is the headquarters of the International Civil Aviation Organization.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Montreal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Trois-Rivières was founded in 1634 and <b>Ville-Marie</b>, later Montreal, in 1642 by Paul de Chomedey de Maisonneuve and Jeanne Mance as a religious mission.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The architectural revivals of the 19th and early 20th centuries produced many churches that imitate elements of St. Peter's, among them St. Josaphat's Basilica in Milwaukee and Mary, Queen of the World Cathedral in <b>Montreal</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/st-peters-basilica">St. Peter's Basilica · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Exhibitions have since gone to Barcelona, Madrid, Bogotá, Santiago de Chile, North America, Sweden, New South Wales, <b>Montreal</b>, New York, Liverpool, Perth and Budapest, and the army has been described as the only set of historic artefacts besides the remains of the Titanic that can draw a crowd…</blockquote><a href="/work/terracotta-army">Terracotta Army · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Many Catholic converts moved to the mission villages of Kahnawake and Kanesatake near <b>Montreal</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/haudenosaunee">Social Hierarchy of the Haudenosaunee</a></li><li><blockquote>The most popular version was written in 1908 by Robert Stanley Weir, a lawyer and Recorder of the City of <b>Montreal</b>; it was not a translation of the French.</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-canada">O Canada</a></li><li><blockquote>Playing for the Boston Bruins against the <b>Montreal</b> Canadiens, Willie O'Ree became the first Black player in the National Hockey League, despite being almost blind in one eye.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-18">18 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
